  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface smoke possible from this afternoon into Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... \nSatellite imagery indicated a plume of wildfire smoke from\nsouthern Quebec down into central New England. The HRRR and RAP\nsmoke models continue to indicate that this could translate to \nsurface smoke in our area from Monday afternoon into Tuesday.\nThis could be noticeable with a slight reduction in the\nvisibility.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nScattered rain showers and thunderstorms from this afternoon \ninto Tuesday, with some locally heavy rainfall possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... \nA series of northern stream shortwaves cross the region this\nafternoon through Tuesday.  At the surface a warm front \napproaches today, lifts through this evening, and followed \nimmediately behind it by a cold front that crosses mainly \novernight and early Tuesday morning. A trough of low pressure \nthen pushes through later Tuesday.\n\nThe strongest of these shortwaves passes through tonight, this \nwill bring the highest chance of showers across all of Eastern \nMaine, with chances this afternoon and Tuesday limited mainly \nacross the North.\n\nWith CAPE this afternoon of only a few hundred J/kg, do not \nexpect any strong storms. Tonight, showalter indices are \nforecast to be around 0 and no CAPE, expect mainly elevated \nconvection, however with some lift ahead of the cold front, \nwould not be surprised if there were a few surface based storms.\nOnce again no strong storms expected. \n\nOn Tuesday do get CAPES up to 500-1000 J/kg across the North,  \nwith mainly southern portions of the area in the left front \nquadrant of a 100+ kt upper jet. This coupled with 30-35kt of \nbulk shear could produce some locally strong storms across the \nNorth, with possibly some strong gusty winds. \n\nPrecipitable waters of 1.25-1.5 inches across the north and\n1.5-1.75 inches elsewhere ahead of the cold front could support\nsome locally heavy rainfall from this afternoon into Tuesday\nmorning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A round of widespread showers and thunderstorms\nexpected Thursday throughout much of the day. A strong storm or\ntwo is possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION... A wave of upper level energy is \nexpected to approach our area from the west Wednesday night and \ndevelop low pressure at the surface which will move into our \narea Thursday bringing a round of widespread showers and \nthunderstorms. Some areas could see a soaking rain based on \nwhere the strongest storms set up. Still uncertain right now as \nguidance is expressing some uncertainty in the position of the \nlow pressure system but the North has the greatest chance with \nthe likelihood of at least 0.5\" of rain around 30%. Around 30-35\nkts of deep-layer wind shear is expected with at least several \nhundred J/kg of instability, although some models show 1000-1500\nJ/kg. Certainly something to watch for rainfall potential and \nstorm threat.",
